  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front will continue to push across the forecast area \ninto early evening. At current writing, the front is roughly \nbisecting the CWA. This front has been responsible for forcing \nshower and thunderstorm development this morning through early \nafternoon, and should continue to bring some showers and storms \nthrough 6pm. Behind, more stable air is working its way in and \nmarks the end of convective potential for the day.\n\nThe convective environment has been characterized by good deep \nlayer shear of 30 to 45 kts and RAP surface CAPE of greater than\n1500 j/kg. Areas that have seen morning cloud cover peel back \nto boost temps towards the lower 80s and complement dew points \nin the upper 60s to around 70 have faired well in storm \nmaintenance. However, dry air aloft has been a limiting factor \nas well as a mid-level inversion (apparent on 12z GYX RAOB). \nThis shouldn't be enough to inhibit gusty to damaging gusts from\nstrong to severe storms, with a lesser and more isolated \nhail/tornado threat.\n\nBehind the cold front tonight, cooler and drier air will invade\nfrom the northwest. Expect some breeze to continue overnight to\nkeep most of the area from decoupling, but CAA should still\nusher in temperatures falling into the low to mid 50s along the\ncoast and interior...and mid to upper 40s across the northern\nmountains/valleys. Otherwise, skies clear with some clouds\nlingering upwind of the mountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nStrong high pressure south of the Great Lakes will gradually\nspread eastward. The high is broad, and will effectively shut \noff moisture transport to the region through the weekend. While \ndaytime temps should still push into seasonable temperatures \nranges, overnight lows should fall a couple degrees below \nnormal. Light winds overnight with mostly clear skies should \nallow for good radiational cooling, and have nudged temperatures\nbelow NBM guidance, particularly Thursday night and Friday \nnight when the high is roughly right overhead. Could also see \nthis linger into Saturday night.",
